            <div xml:id="d921">

                <pb facs="0945" n="833" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg833"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 22918/7.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Lorillard to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Lisbon</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1910-01-31"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">January 31, 1910</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 598.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In continuation of my No. 595<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d921-f1"/> of the 25th instant, relative to the arrest and
                    imprisonment of Antonio Silveira Nunes, jr., a minor son of a naturalized
                    American citizen, for nonperformance of military service, I have the honor to
                    report that on the 26th instant the minister for foreign affairs verbally
                    informed me that orders for Nunes’s release had been cabled to Terceira on the
                    previous Monday, as he had promised me would be done in his interview with me of
                    the 23d instant. On the 27th the minister confirmed this information in a formal
                    note, a copy and a translation of which are herewith <pb facs="0946" n="834"
                        xml:id="pg834"/> inclosed. In view of section 2 of article 22 of the
                    Portuguese Civil Code, a copy and a translation of which are inclosed herewith,
                    the note expresses the hope that the apparent conflict between the American and
                    Portuguese laws on the subject of the nationality of minor children of
                    naturalized citizens may be settled to the satisfaction of both Governments.</p>
                <p>In my reply, a copy of which is also inclosed, I informed the minister that I
                    would transmit you a copy of his note for such action as you may desire to take
                    on the question.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George Lorillard.</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d921-a1.xml" xpointer="d921-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d921-a2.xml" xpointer="d921-a2"/>
